    {
      "id": "sec-002-background",
      "number": null,
      "title": "Background",
      "level": 1,
      "pageStart": 10,
      "pageEnd": 14,
      "labelStart": "2",
      "labelEnd": "6",
      "words": 1476,
      "summary": {
        "text": "Defines digital health and explains that guidelines are usually narrative, so translating them into digital systems is costly and inconsistent, and system content is often undocumented or proprietary. It introduces digital adaptation kits as a way to package WHO recommendations into standard, transparent requirements within the SMART Guidelines approach. It also explains digital tracking and decision support and the stages from paper to smart digital systems.",
        "status": "draft"
      },
      "keywords": [
        {
          "term": "digital tracking",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "digital adaptation",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "digital systems",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "digital health",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "digital tools",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "content requirements",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "decision-support algorithms",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "digital intervention",
          "heading": false
        }
      ],
      "extract": "Digital health – defined broadly as the systematic application of information and communications technologies, computer science and data to support informed decision-making by individuals, the health workforce and health systems, to strengthen resilience to disease and improve health and wellness (1) – is increasingly being applied as an essential enabler for health service delivery and accountability. Ministries of",
      "extractCut": true
    },
    {
      "id": "sec-003-objectives",
      "number": null,
      "title": "Objectives",
      "level": 1,
      "pageStart": 14,
      "pageEnd": 15,
      "labelStart": "6",
      "labelEnd": "7",
      "words": 342,
      "summary": {
        "text": "Sets out the objectives of the antenatal care digital adaptation kit: providing a shared language for programme managers, developers and implementers and ensuring digital tracking and decision-support systems follow WHO guidance. It notes links to other kits, including a forthcoming one on self-care.",
        "status": "draft"
      },
      "keywords": [
        {
          "term": "dak",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "software developers",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "health content",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "self-care interventions",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "digital systems",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "anc",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "health programme",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "antenatal care",
          "heading": false
        }
      ],
      "extract": "This DAK focuses on antenatal care (ANC) and aims to provide a common language across various audiences – maternal health and other programme managers, software developers, and implementers of digital systems – to ensure a common understanding of the appropriate health information content within the ANC health programme area, as a mechanism to catalyse the effective use of these digital systems. The key objectives",
      "extractCut": true
    },
    {
      "id": "sec-004-components-of-a-digital-adaptation-kit",
      "number": null,
      "title": "Components of a digital adaptation kit",
      "level": 1,
      "pageStart": 15,
      "pageEnd": 19,
      "labelStart": "7",
      "labelEnd": "11",
      "words": 1319,
      "summary": {
        "text": "Describes the eight interlinked components of the digital adaptation kit, from health interventions and recommendations, personas and user scenarios to workflows, core data elements, decision-support logic, indicators and functional requirements. A table explains the purpose, outputs and adaptation needed for each component. It also notes the kit builds on PATH's work on maternal health information system requirements and explains naming conventions for requirement identifiers.",
        "status": "draft"
      },
      "keywords": [
        {
          "term": "adaptation kit",
          "heading": true
        },
        {
          "term": "digital adaptation",
          "heading": true
        },
        {
          "term": "decision-support logic",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "non-functional requirement",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "decision-support table",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "generic business",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "generic personas",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "functional requirement",
          "heading": false
        }
      ],
      "extract": "The DAK comprises eight interlinked components: (1) health interventions and associated recommendations; (2) generic personas; (3) user scenarios; (4) generic business processes and workflows; (5) core data elements; (6) decision-support logic; (7) indicators and reporting requirements; and (8) high- level functional and non-functional requirements. Table 1 provides an overview of each of the contributing components",
      "extractCut": true
    },
    {
      "id": "sec-005-how-to-use-this-digital-adaptation-kit",
      "number": null,
      "title": "How to use this digital adaptation kit",
      "level": 1,
      "pageStart": 19,
      "pageEnd": 22,
      "labelStart": "11",
      "labelEnd": "14",
      "words": 1152,
      "summary": {
        "text": "Identifies the main audience as ministry of health programme managers working with digital and information system teams, along with business analysts who translate guidance into system requirements. It describes the modular design linked to other health areas and scenarios for use, such as checking existing systems against WHO content or building a new point-of-care client record. It also explains terminology such as the use of \"client\" and \"woman\".",
        "status": "draft"
      },
